From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:20:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/pgssubdec: ignore an end segment that follows no
 presentation segment

A PGS display set begins with its presentation composition segment and
ends with its end segment. decode() outputs a subtitle at every end
segment, from the composition kept in the context, whether a
presentation segment came before it or not. An end segment that follows
none outputs the last composition again:

- Some Matroska files store a copy of each end segment in a block of its
  own, at the time of its display set (FFmpeg's pgs_frame_merge also
  passes such a segment through as a packet of its own). Each subtitle
  then comes out twice with one time, and a player that keeps the
  subtitles that start at one time, as Kodi does, draws the picture twice
  over itself, which blends its semi-transparent pixels twice.
- An end segment in front of a display set in one packet outputs the
  last composition, and the display set after it is then dropped as a
  "Duplicate display segment": the old subtitle is shown, not the new.
- After a seek in a stream of one segment per packet (MPEG-TS, as on a
  Blu-ray, or SUP), the end segment of a display set whose presentation
  segment was before the seek outputs a composition from before the
  seek, at a time after it.

Remember in the context that a presentation segment was read, and output
a subtitle at an end segment only then; the end segment uses it up. The
flag is kept in the context because the segments of one display set
come in separate packets in MPEG-TS and SUP. A flush clears it, so that
a presentation segment read before a seek is not completed by an end
segment read after it. It is set before the presentation segment is
parsed, so that one that does not parse still outputs at its end
segment what was read, as before. A second end segment in the packet of
its display set is now skipped like any other, instead of being
reported as a duplicate (which with err_recognition explode dropped the
subtitle). The first end segment skipped is logged, once, as a warning.

This follows matthane's patch for CoreELEC (commit 0e28918fc1ca there,
"ffmpeg: add patch to ignore lone PGS end segments"); here the
presentation segment is checked before the duplicate, and the warning is
new.

Tested with libavcodec built from this tree with every patch of the
image, plain and with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
with packets given as Kodi gives them, of streams written segment by
segment in one packet per display set and in one packet per segment,
and through FFmpeg's Matroska and M2TS muxers and demuxers. A copy of
each end segment in a packet of its own gave 7 subtitles for 4 display
sets before and gives 4 now, byte for byte those of the stream without
the copies. An end segment in front of a display set, the segments of a
display set without its presentation segment, and the two seeks above
gave a subtitle from an earlier composition before (and, the first, lost
the new one) and give what the stream without those segments gives now.
Streams without such a segment (display sets that show, clear and
update a palette, with a forced and a cropped object, an object in three
segments, seven kinds of damaged display set, two display sets in one
packet), in both layouts and with and without forced_subs_only and
explode, give the same subtitles, return values and log lines before
and after.
---
 libavcodec/pgssubdec.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libavcodec/pgssubdec.c b/libavcodec/pgssubdec.c
index 65d929b..354a297 100644
--- a/libavcodec/pgssubdec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/pgssubdec.c
@@ -97,6 +97,8 @@ typedef struct PGSSubContext {
     PGSSubPalettes     palettes;
     PGSSubObjects      objects;
     int forced_subs_only;
+    int composition_pending;
+    int lone_end_logged;
 } PGSSubContext;
 
 static void flush_cache(AVCodecContext *avctx)
@@ -149,6 +151,15 @@ static av_cold int close_decoder(AVCodecContext *avctx)
     return 0;
 }
 
+static void flush_decoder(AVCodecContext *avctx)
+{
+    PGSSubContext *ctx = avctx->priv_data;
+
+    /* A presentation segment read before a seek is not completed by an end
+     * segment read after it. */
+    ctx->composition_pending = 0;
+}
+
 /**
  * Decode the RLE data.
  *
@@ -618,6 +629,7 @@ static int display_end_segment(AVCodecContext *avctx, AVSubtitle *sub,
 static int decode(AVCodecContext *avctx, AVSubtitle *sub,
                   int *got_sub_ptr, const AVPacket *avpkt)
 {
+    PGSSubContext *ctx = avctx->priv_data;
     const uint8_t *buf = avpkt->data;
     int buf_size       = avpkt->size;
 
@@ -664,6 +676,9 @@ static int decode(AVCodecContext *avctx, AVSubtitle *sub,
             ret = parse_object_segment(avctx, buf, segment_length);
             break;
         case PRESENTATION_SEGMENT:
+            /* Also when the segment does not parse: the end segment then
+             * outputs what was read, as before. */
+            ctx->composition_pending = 1;
             ret = parse_presentation_segment(avctx, buf, segment_length, sub->pts);
             break;
         case WINDOW_SEGMENT:
@@ -677,6 +692,19 @@ static int decode(AVCodecContext *avctx, AVSubtitle *sub,
              */
             break;
         case DISPLAY_SEGMENT:
+            /* An end segment completes the display set that a presentation
+             * segment began. One that follows none, such as the copy of each
+             * end segment that some Matroska files store in a block of its
+             * own, would output the last composition again. */
+            if (!ctx->composition_pending) {
+                if (!ctx->lone_end_logged) {
+                    av_log(avctx, AV_LOG_WARNING, "Ignoring end segments that "
+                           "follow no presentation segment\n");
+                    ctx->lone_end_logged = 1;
+                }
+                break;
+            }
+            ctx->composition_pending = 0;
             if (*got_sub_ptr) {
                 av_log(avctx, AV_LOG_ERROR, "Duplicate display segment\n");
                 ret = AVERROR_INVALIDDATA;
@@ -724,6 +752,7 @@ const FFCodec ff_pgssub_decoder = {
     .priv_data_size = sizeof(PGSSubContext),
     .init           = init_decoder,
     .close          = close_decoder,
+    .flush          = flush_decoder,
     FF_CODEC_DECODE_SUB_CB(decode),
     .p.priv_class   = &pgsdec_class,
 };
